# Adding Custom Fields to Custom Records Across SuiteApps in NetSuite

When working on SuiteApp A and needing to add a custom field to a custom record defined in SuiteApp B, there are a few approaches you can take:

## Option 1: Using SuiteScript 2.0 Record API

You can programmatically create custom fields using the `N/record` module:

```javascript
/**
 * @NApiVersion 2.1
 * @NModuleScope SameAccount
 */
define(['N/record'], function(record) {

    function createCustomField() {
        // Create a custom field on the custom record type from SuiteApp B
        var customFieldRecord = record.create({
            type: record.Type.CUSTOM_FIELD,
            isDynamic: true
        });

        customFieldRecord.setValue({
            fieldId: 'rectype',
            // Reference the custom record type ID from SuiteApp B
            value: 'customrecord_suiteb_recordtype' 
        });

        customFieldRecord.setValue({
            fieldId: 'label',
            value: 'My Custom Field from SuiteApp A'
        });

        customFieldRecord.setValue({
            fieldId: 'id',
            value: 'custrecord_suitea_field'
        });

        customFieldRecord.setValue({
            fieldId: 'fieldtype',
            value: 'TEXT' // Or other field type as needed
        });

        // Set other required properties
        customFieldRecord.setValue({fieldId: 'displaytype', value: 'NORMAL'});
        customFieldRecord.setValue({fieldId: 'store', value: 'T'});

        var fieldId = customFieldRecord.save();
        return fieldId;
    }

    return {
        createCustomField: createCustomField
    };
});
```

## Option 2: SuiteApp Installation Script

Include a script in your SuiteApp A's installation process that creates the custom field:

```javascript
/**
 * @NApiVersion 2.1
 * @NScriptType BundleInstallationScript
 */
define(['N/record'], function(record) {

    function afterInstall(params) {
        // Create the custom field on SuiteApp B's custom record
        var customFieldRecord = record.create({
            type: record.Type.CUSTOM_FIELD,
            isDynamic: true
        });

        // Set all the necessary field values as in the previous example
        // ...

        var fieldId = customFieldRecord.save();
        log.debug('Custom field created', fieldId);
    }

    return {
        afterInstall: afterInstall
    };
});
```

## Option 3: SDF (SuiteCloud Development Framework)

Include the custom field definition in your SuiteApp A's SDF project:

```xml
<customfield scriptid="custrecord_suitea_field">
  <accesslevel>2</accesslevel>
  <applyformatting>F</applyformatting>
  <checkspelling>F</checkspelling>
  <defaultchecked>F</defaultchecked>
  <defaultvalue></defaultvalue>
  <description></description>
  <displaytype>NORMAL</displaytype>
  <encryptatrest>F</encryptatrest>
  <fieldtype>TEXT</fieldtype>
  <label>My Custom Field from SuiteApp A</label>
  <searchlevel>2</searchlevel>
  <storevalue>T</storevalue>
  <valuechecked></valuechecked>
  <valueunchecked></valueunchecked>
  <!-- Reference the custom record type from SuiteApp B -->
  <customrecordtype>[SuiteApp B's custom record ID]</customrecordtype>
</customfield>
```

## Important Considerations

1. **Permissions**: Your SuiteApp A will need sufficient permissions to create custom fields.

2. **Dependency Management**: You should explicitly declare the dependency on SuiteApp B in your SuiteApp A's manifest file.

3. **Versioning**: Consider what happens during updates to either SuiteApp.

4. **Error Handling**: Include logic to handle cases where the custom record from SuiteApp B doesn't exist yet.

This approach allows your SuiteApp A to extend the functionality of custom records from SuiteApp B while maintaining proper separation between the two applications.
